=== Fact-checking ===
The [[Okaiken Press Fact-Check Initiative]] evaluated several claims made during the debate:

* '''Honda's claim of "99.8% safety effectiveness"''': '''Mostly False'''. The figure referred to the automated door sensors' success rate in laboratory conditions, not real-world operations. Actual incident data showed a 97.3% effectiveness rate, with the gap representing hundreds of near-misses annually.

* '''Sadanuma's claim about the gold standard''': '''False'''. The LDP platform did not propose returning to a gold standard, but rather a "commodity-backed stability fund." Sadanuma mischaracterized his own party's policy.

* '''Nakayama's claim about Wellness Voucher usage''': '''Misleading'''. While vouchers could technically be used for gym memberships and therapy, the program's administrative complexity meant that only 23% of issued vouchers had been successfully redeemed.

* '''Ikazaki's claim about "bread lines in Seto"''': '''Mostly True'''. Food assistance applications in Seto Prefecture had increased 340% since January 2026, with some distribution centers reporting queues of over 200 people.

== Historical significance ==
The debate is widely regarded by political historians as a turning point in the 2026 election campaign. Ikazaki's performance transformed the race from a two-way contest between the OSP and LDP into a genuine four-way battle. The SCP's subsequent surge would have profound implications for coalition negotiations following the election.

The debate also marked the effective end of Nakayama's momentum. His "mental toughness" comment alienated key constituencies and provided ammunition for both the LDP and SCP to attack his fitness-influencer background as evidence of unseriousness.

Political scientist {{nihongo|Yamamoto Keiko|山本恵子}} of [[Okami University]] wrote in a post-election analysis: "The April 30 debate was the moment the 2026 election stopped being a referendum on Honda Shigetsuma and became something far more complex—a contest over the soul of the Sanese left, with Ikazaki positioning himself as the authentic voice of working-class grievance against Nakayama's wellness-focused populism."

The debate's minimalist production design also influenced subsequent election broadcasts in Okaiken, with multiple networks abandoning the traditional "information-dense" aesthetic in favor of cleaner presentations that emphasized candidate presence.

